<h1>Selection Sort</h1>
<p>
    Selection sort loops the array and finds the smallest element 
    by checking if each one is smaller than the current smallest one.
    After reaching the end of the array it swaps the first element with the smallest one.
    Then it repeats the process again, starting starting one element ahead of where it started before.
    <br>
    <br>
    Because it always swaps the next smallest element into place, 
    Selection Sort writes to the array the fewest number of time possible.
    <br>
    <br>
    Similar to <a href="/algorithm?id=Bubble">Bubble Sort</a>, 
    Selection Sort known for its simplicity, but also its slowness.
    Selection Sort always takes the same amount of time, 
    whether the array is already sorted, is almost sorted, or unsorted.
    Selection Sort is faster than <a href="/algorithm?id=Bubble">Bubble Sort</a> on average, 
    but <a href="/algorithm?id=Bubble">Bubble Sort</a> 
    will run faster on sorted or almost sorted arrays.
    It is used when a simple to write algorithm is needed, not a fast one.
</p>

